  "answer_text": "Whether Crimea belongs to Russia depends on the distinction between **international law (*de jure*)** and **actual physical control (*de facto*)**. \n\n### 1. International Law (*De Jure*): Crimea belongs to Ukraine\nAccording to international law and the vast majority of the international community, **Crimea is recognized as a part of Ukraine**. \n* **The UN Position:** Following the Russian annexation of Crimea in March 2014, the United Nations General Assembly adopted Resolution 68/262, which affirmed Ukraine\u2019s territorial integrity and declared the referendum held in Crimea invalid. \n* **Global Recognition:** The United States, the European Union, Turkey, and most other nations do not recognize the annexation and officially view Crimea as temporarily occupied Ukrainian territory. \n\n### 2. Actual Control (*De Facto*): Crimea is controlled by Russia\nIn practice, **Russia has exercised full physical, political, and administrative control** over the Crimean Peninsula since 2014. \n* **Administration:** Russia administers Crimea as two of its own federal subjects: the Republic of Crimea and the federal city of Sevastopol. \n* **Integration:** Since the annexation, Russia has integrated Crimea's legal system, currency (the Russian ruble), time zone, telecommunications, and infrastructure (such as the Crimean Bridge linking the peninsula to mainland Russia).\n\n### The Context of the Dispute\n* **The 2014 Annexation:** Following the ouster of Ukraine's pro-Russian President Viktor Yanukovych during the Maidan protests in early 2014, Russian forces entered Crimea and seized government buildings. A hastily arranged referendum was held, in which authorities claimed 97% of voters supported joining Russia. International bodies and Ukraine rejected the vote, noting it was held at gunpoint under military occupation, violated Ukraine's constitution, and lacked independent international monitors.\n* **The Russian View:** Moscow claims Crimea is historically Russian land (having been part of the Russian Empire and later the Soviet Union, before being transferred to the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic in 1954). Russia asserts that the 2014 referendum was a legitimate exercise of self-determination.\n* **Ongoing Conflict:** Ukraine maintains that the restoration of its 1991 borders\u2014which includes the liberation of Crimea\u2014is a non-negotiable condition for ending the ongoing Russia-Ukraine war. The peninsula remains a major front in the war, with Ukraine regularly striking Russian military targets and logistics hubs located there.",
